   "tutor": "The rule: use at with clock times, on with days and dates, and in with months and years.\nRight: \"The film starts at six o'clock.\" Wrong: \"The film starts in six o'clock.\" The wrong one uses in, which goes with months and years, not clock times.\nWorked example: \"The zoo is closed on Saturday.\" First, I find the time word: Saturday. Next, I ask what kind it is: a day. Days take on.\nNow try the same steps. Write in, on or at: My train leaves ___ 5 o'clock.",
